Compile a guest source in the VFS into an RV32 ELF.

usage: @compile <src> [<out>]

Reads a guest source from an absolute path in the in-world filesystem
(the VFS) and produces an RV32 ELF in the VFS. With no <out> the image is
written to /bin/<name>.elf, where <name> is the source leaf without its
extension. An explicit <out> must be an absolute VFS path.

The language is chosen by the source extension:
  .c          C
  .s, .S      assembly
  .pas, .kp   Kobold-Pascal

A compiled C or assembly program must supply main(); a Kobold program is a
normal `program ... begin ... end.` The result is a plain program, the same
shape as the SDK examples, not a command verb. The source path must be
absolute, for example /src/tool.c, and the source must already exist in the
VFS at that path. Use @fedit to author one.

The compile runs the skjegg toolchain as RV32 guests under the embedded
runtime, each confined to a temporary build directory. It needs no host
process execution, so it works with the security sandbox active. Compiler
errors are shown to you: a failed stage reports its diagnostics.

To launch the result, bind it with @program and run it with @run.

Wizard only. A compiled program runs code with its owner's authority.

Example:
  @compile /src/tool.c
      Compile /src/tool.c to /bin/tool.elf.
  @compile /src/hello.pas /bin/hello.elf
      Compile a Kobold-Pascal source to an explicit output path.
